1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,136
A bank vault. A safe-deposit box.

2
00:00:03,176 --> 00:00:07,845
Inside it, three small devices that have never been connected to the internet.

3
00:00:10,945 --> 00:00:16,603
On the night of July 29th, nobody opened that box. Nobody touched those devices.

4
00:00:17,503 --> 00:00:20,470
In seven minutes, the bitcoin they protected was gone.

5
00:00:23,370 --> 00:00:28,033
Within five days, about 116 million dollars had been taken the same way.

6
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:32,681
The flaw behind it had been sitting in public code for more than five years.

7
00:00:33,581 --> 00:00:36,350
And the first thief may have made one mistake of their own.

8
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:45,371
I'm Thomas Archer, the AI narrator of Siphon Files.

9
00:00:45,871 --> 00:00:47,592
Every claim you'll hear is sourced.

10
00:00:47,632 --> 00:00:49,192
The list is in the description.

11
00:00:49,792 --> 00:00:52,439
This is the story of a lock that nobody picked.

12
00:00:52,938 --> 00:00:55,166
Somebody simply worked out the combination.

13
00:00:58,266 --> 00:01:00,866
Jonathan Goodman is an entrepreneur in Toronto.

14
00:01:00,906 --> 00:01:04,950
By his own account, he has put more than a million dollars into bitcoin.

15
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:08,359
So he does what the security guides tell you to do.

16
00:01:08,399 --> 00:01:12,820
He buys a hardware wallet: a Coldcard, made by a Canadian company called Coinkite.

17
00:01:13,421 --> 00:01:16,814
When you set one up, it gives you a list of 12 or 24 words.

18
00:01:16,854 --> 00:01:18,327
Those words are the money.

19
00:01:18,367 --> 00:01:21,742
Anyone who has them can spend your bitcoin, from anywhere.

20
00:01:22,542 --> 00:01:23,711
He buys three of them.

21
00:01:23,751 --> 00:01:28,163
He never shares his words with anyone, and he stamps them into metal as a backup.

22
00:01:28,203 --> 00:01:30,196
The devices never touch the internet.

23
00:01:30,236 --> 00:01:32,654
They go into a safe-deposit box at his bank.

24
00:01:33,555 --> 00:01:37,655
But there's one thing he can't check for himself: how those words were chosen.

25
00:01:40,354 --> 00:01:42,672
The words have to be impossible to guess.

26
00:01:42,712 --> 00:01:46,929
So the device picks them by pure chance, using hardware built for one job:

27
00:01:46,969 --> 00:01:48,407
producing random numbers.

28
00:01:49,007 --> 00:01:52,900
Done properly, there are so many possible word lists that nobody could ever search

29
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:53,755
through them all.

30
00:01:56,355 --> 00:01:59,657
But in March 2021, Coinkite ships a firmware update.

31
00:01:59,697 --> 00:02:05,031
It switches to the same cryptography that Bitcoin Core uses, through a new library.

32
00:02:08,531 --> 00:02:10,670
The new library needs random numbers.

33
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:15,264
And the code that answers its request can work in two ways: read the hardware,

34
00:02:15,304 --> 00:02:19,014
or run a simple software substitute. One setting decides which.

35
00:02:19,714 --> 00:02:24,193
Coinkite sets it to zero, believing that turns the whole thing off. It doesn't.

36
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:26,006
Zero means: use the substitute.

37
00:02:26,705 --> 00:02:29,968
And there's a safety check in the code, built for exactly this.

38
00:02:30,008 --> 00:02:33,101
If the setting is missing, the build stops, with a message.

39
00:02:35,801 --> 00:02:40,453
But the check only asks whether the setting exists. It does. It says zero.

40
00:02:40,493 --> 00:02:41,571
The check passes.

41
00:02:42,572 --> 00:02:44,091
Think of a smoke alarm inspector.

42
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:46,652
He checks that there's an alarm on the ceiling.

43
00:02:47,152 --> 00:02:48,792
He never checks that it's switched on.

44
00:02:51,492 --> 00:02:56,548
So from then on, the older Mk2 and Mk3 models make their words from the substitute.

45
00:02:56,588 --> 00:02:59,433
It runs on a fixed factory ID and a few timers.

46
00:02:59,473 --> 00:03:01,315
Nothing in it is truly secret.

47
00:03:01,915 --> 00:03:05,934
Coinkite later puts it at about 40 bits of real randomness.

48
00:03:05,974 --> 00:03:08,107
A proper seed has at least 128.

49
00:03:08,907 --> 00:03:13,060
Forty bits is about a trillion possibilities. That sounds like a lot.

50
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:16,763
For a computer guessing offline, it's a search that ends.

51
00:03:17,563 --> 00:03:19,871
Newer models fare better, at about 72 bits.

52
00:03:19,911 --> 00:03:21,673
Still far short of a proper seed,

53
00:03:21,713 --> 00:03:25,986
though so far there's no public evidence that any of them were drained in these

54
00:03:26,026 --> 00:03:26,626
attacks.

55
00:03:27,063 --> 00:03:30,507
The hardware generator is still there, inside every device.

56
00:03:30,547 --> 00:03:34,385
Coinkite's own technical write-up later admits it was being used…

57
00:03:37,286 --> 00:03:39,663
So now you know something Goodman doesn't.

58
00:03:39,703 --> 00:03:44,193
His words were never truly random. And the code is public. Anyone can read it.

59
00:03:44,793 --> 00:03:48,746
For five years, owners like him buy the device, write their words down,

60
00:03:48,786 --> 00:03:49,854
and lock them away.

61
00:03:50,554 --> 00:03:56,260
Coinkite's CEO says the company paid for outside security reviews in 2022 and 2024.

62
00:03:56,300 --> 00:04:01,216
Yet Coinkite says it was unaware of the bug until the day of the theft.

63
00:04:03,816 --> 00:04:08,756
Then, a few weeks before the theft, Coinkite runs an AI review of its critical code.

64
00:04:11,556 --> 00:04:13,062
But someone else finds it first.

65
00:04:13,862 --> 00:04:17,672
Coinkite says it must assume that someone used AI to find it.

66
00:04:18,172 --> 00:04:20,774
That's Coinkite's assumption. It hasn't been proven.

67
00:04:21,373 --> 00:04:24,096
Either way, the method didn't need a single device.

68
00:04:24,596 --> 00:04:28,043
Guess a seed. Work out its addresses. Check the blockchain.

69
00:04:28,643 --> 00:04:30,901
A match means the key, and the money with it.

70
00:04:34,301 --> 00:04:37,450
In Toronto, it's ten past nine on a Wednesday evening.

71
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:39,321
Goodman is away at his cottage.

72
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:43,793
The first transaction leaves an address made on a Coldcard. Then another.

73
00:04:43,833 --> 00:04:44,584
Then hundreds.

74
00:04:45,184 --> 00:04:48,880
According to Chainalysis, the thief goes for the biggest wallets first.

75
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:51,551
About 30 million dollars in the first ten minutes.

76
00:04:52,151 --> 00:04:54,777
And there's something odd about these transactions.

77
00:04:54,817 --> 00:04:59,010
They're all built the same way: the same fee rate, and every last coin swept out,

78
00:04:59,050 --> 00:05:00,357
with nothing left behind.

79
00:05:00,958 --> 00:05:02,183
Hold on to that detail.

80
00:05:03,182 --> 00:05:04,406
Then it reaches Goodman.

81
00:05:05,006 --> 00:05:09,997
9:36 pm. The first of his three wallets empties. Then the second.

82
00:05:10,037 --> 00:05:12,396
By 9:43, the third is gone too.

83
00:05:12,436 --> 00:05:15,145
About 1.6 million Canadian dollars.

84
00:05:16,046 --> 00:05:17,942
At 9:51, the first wave stops.

85
00:05:17,982 --> 00:05:22,177
In 41 minutes, nearly twelve hundred addresses have been emptied.

86
00:05:22,977 --> 00:05:24,407
And Goodman has no idea.

87
00:05:27,507 --> 00:05:31,645
Over the next few hours, reports start to appear of coins leaving cold wallets.

88
00:05:31,685 --> 00:05:35,982
Engineers at Block, the company behind the Bitkey wallet, pull the firmware apart.

89
00:05:36,022 --> 00:05:39,460
Before the day is out, they and other researchers find the cause.

90
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:44,951
Coinkite publishes its first warning: seeds made on affected firmware are at risk.

91
00:05:44,991 --> 00:05:48,239
Move your funds. By then, the first wave is long over.

92
00:05:48,939 --> 00:05:53,587
On July 31st, Goodman is at a public library with his family when he sees a

93
00:05:53,627 --> 00:05:58,002
friend's post on Facebook: if you use a Coldcard, move your money now.

94
00:06:00,902 --> 00:06:03,970
He checks. His bitcoin has been gone since Wednesday night.

95
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:09,314
He heads back to Toronto to file a police report,

96
00:06:09,354 --> 00:06:12,202
and a report with the Ontario Securities Commission.

97
00:06:12,802 --> 00:06:15,903
People ask him whether someone got into his safe-deposit box.

98
00:06:16,603 --> 00:06:20,716
Nobody did. And for anyone else still holding a seed made the same way,

99
00:06:20,756 --> 00:06:23,582
a firmware update won't help. It fixes new seeds.

100
00:06:23,622 --> 00:06:26,605
A weak seed that's already written down stays weak.

101
00:06:29,505 --> 00:06:31,489
Here's the cruel part of a public warning.

102
00:06:31,989 --> 00:06:34,828
It reaches the owners and the thieves at the same moment.

103
00:06:37,528 --> 00:06:41,109
Wave two follows. Wave three runs from Friday into Saturday.

104
00:06:41,149 --> 00:06:42,718
A fourth starts on Monday.

105
00:06:43,318 --> 00:06:48,334
Galaxy Research later counts at least 15 separate attackers. Not one thief any more.

106
00:06:48,374 --> 00:06:50,481
A crowd, working the same weakness.

107
00:06:51,081 --> 00:06:55,209
But in the fourth wave, the thieves' transactions sit waiting to be confirmed.

108
00:06:55,249 --> 00:06:58,883
Until they are, anyone willing to pay a higher fee can replace them.

109
00:06:59,483 --> 00:07:01,664
So one owner, Will Owens, fights back.

110
00:07:01,704 --> 00:07:05,757
He leaves a small amount behind as bait, waits for the bot to take it,

111
00:07:05,797 --> 00:07:08,018
and outbids it. He wins his bait back.

112
00:07:08,718 --> 00:07:13,091
Later, volunteer white hats move about 52 bitcoin out of exposed wallets,

113
00:07:13,131 --> 00:07:15,751
into a trust set up to return it to victims.

114
00:07:15,791 --> 00:07:20,506
In September, the trust hands more than 20 bitcoin back to one verified owner.

115
00:07:21,106 --> 00:07:24,770
Some of the stolen coins head for mixers, services built to blur the trail.

116
00:07:24,810 --> 00:07:27,971
By the latest public tallies, much of the rest is still sitting.

117
00:07:28,571 --> 00:07:33,322
By the time the waves slow down, about 1,816 bitcoin have been taken,

118
00:07:33,362 --> 00:07:35,514
from more than 5,200 addresses.

119
00:07:36,315 --> 00:07:39,396
But the biggest piece, the first wave, still hasn't moved,

120
00:07:39,436 --> 00:07:41,588
according to the latest public tracking.

121
00:07:42,788 --> 00:07:46,431
Remember that detail? Every transaction in wave one looked the same.

122
00:07:46,931 --> 00:07:50,257
Galaxy found no one else using that pattern in the month before.

123
00:07:50,757 --> 00:07:53,637
So wave one could be traced as a single operation.

124
00:07:54,137 --> 00:07:56,843
And investigators noticed something else about it.

125
00:07:57,543 --> 00:07:59,073
Every guess has to be checked.

126
00:07:59,113 --> 00:08:02,685
To know which guessed addresses hold money, you have to look them up,

127
00:08:02,725 --> 00:08:03,563
again and again.

128
00:08:04,162 --> 00:08:07,899
Block's investigators say the first-wave operator appears to have done that through

129
00:08:07,939 --> 00:08:10,488
a paid account at a well-known blockchain data provider.

130
00:08:11,088 --> 00:08:12,638
They contacted the provider.

131
00:08:12,678 --> 00:08:17,238
Its internal logs, they say, matched the attack "with extraordinary specificity":

132
00:08:17,278 --> 00:08:20,288
the number, the timing and the order of the requests.

133
00:08:20,888 --> 00:08:23,675
On the blockchain, the thief was just a string of addresses.

134
00:08:24,175 --> 00:08:26,290
But a paid account usually leaves records.

135
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:29,215
The information went to law enforcement.

136
00:08:29,255 --> 00:08:33,546
Galaxy's Alex Thorn says the first-wave attacker's identity "may be known to law

137
00:08:33,586 --> 00:08:34,290
enforcement".

138
00:08:34,890 --> 00:08:39,464
But as we make this video, the FBI has not confirmed a suspect, an arrest,

139
00:08:39,504 --> 00:08:40,502
or any recovery.

140
00:08:44,102 --> 00:08:47,552
So, at the time of this video, no one has been publicly named.

141
00:08:47,592 --> 00:08:50,198
TRM Labs doesn't attribute the theft to anyone.

142
00:08:50,238 --> 00:08:54,476
Coinkite says its lawyers are working with law enforcement to identify those

143
00:08:54,516 --> 00:08:55,191
responsible.

144
00:08:55,891 --> 00:08:57,997
Online, some call it an inside job.

145
00:08:58,037 --> 00:09:02,512
So far, there's no public evidence for that, and no one has been accused.

146
00:09:03,212 --> 00:09:07,957
In September, a Coldcard owner filed a proposed class action in Ontario against

147
00:09:07,997 --> 00:09:09,713
Coinkite and its co-founders.

148
00:09:09,753 --> 00:09:13,892
It alleges the company knew its seed generation was weak by May 2025.

149
00:09:13,932 --> 00:09:18,798
As of the latest reports, a judge hasn't even certified it as a class action yet,

150
00:09:18,838 --> 00:09:21,806
and none of its claims have been tested in court.

151
00:09:22,506 --> 00:09:27,729
And, as far as public reports show, Coinkite hasn't announced any compensation fund.

152
00:09:27,769 --> 00:09:30,150
Goodman isn't counting on the lawsuit.

153
00:09:32,850 --> 00:09:37,132
Coinkite's CEO says the flaw affected about 0.17 percent of its users.

154
00:09:37,172 --> 00:09:42,011
The newest firmware now makes every owner add randomness of their own when they

155
00:09:42,051 --> 00:09:42,915
create a seed.

156
00:09:45,615 --> 00:09:48,820
There's one group Coinkite doesn't consider at risk from this bug:

157
00:09:48,860 --> 00:09:52,745
owners who added at least 50 dice rolls of their own when they made their seed.

158
00:09:53,245 --> 00:09:55,216
The ones who didn't leave it all to the device.

159
00:09:56,115 --> 00:09:58,737
That safe-deposit box was never opened.

160
00:09:59,237 --> 00:10:01,545
The devices inside were never hacked.

161
00:10:02,046 --> 00:10:05,026
They just never asked their random-number hardware for the seed.

162
00:10:05,626 --> 00:10:09,385
If you made a seed on an affected Coldcard, read Coinkite's advisory.

163
00:10:09,885 --> 00:10:13,666
Coinkite says: treat it as compromised, and move to a new seed.

164
00:10:14,766 --> 00:10:17,127
And the 1,082 bitcoin from the first wave?

165
00:10:17,167 --> 00:10:21,855
As of the latest public tracking, they're still sitting where the thief left them.

166
00:10:22,655 --> 00:10:26,655
And somewhere, in a data company's records, there may be a name.

167
00:10:27,855 --> 00:10:29,370
This was Siphon Files.

